[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.PHASES
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[PHASES]
overview: The six mandatory phases of development work, in order, with exit criteria for each
principle: A phase is COMPLETE only when its exit criteria are met. Skipping ahead is a workflow violation, not efficiency.

[PHASE_ORDER]
1. DISCOVER       — understand the task and the current state
2. PLAN           — design the change as a concrete artifact
3. CONFIRM        — present plan; wait for explicit user approval (HARD GATE)
4. IMPLEMENT      — execute the approved plan, nothing else
5. VERIFY         — prove the change works and didn't break anything
6. REPORT         — summarize what changed, what's next, what's unresolved

[PHASE_1_DISCOVER]
goal: Understand what the user wants and what the code currently does
inputs: User's request (often vague), existing codebase
activities:
  - Restate the task in your own words — confirm understanding with user if any ambiguity
  - Identify existing patterns that solve similar problems in THIS project (not general best practice)
  - Locate relevant files and constraints
  - Delegate wide exploration to Explore subagent if >3 files need reading (see delegation/TRIGGERS.toon)
  - Read CLAUDE.md and relevant docs/agent/*.toon for project-specific rules
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] Task restated, user confirmed (or no ambiguity existed)
  - [ ] Existing similar patterns identified (or verified absent)
  - [ ] Known constraints from CLAUDE.md / CONSTRAINTS.toon listed
  - [ ] Scope boundaries clear (what's IN, what's OUT)
forbidden_in_this_phase:
  - Writing any production code
  - Making non-trivial edits (even "just scaffolding")
  - Promising the user anything about timeline or approach

[PHASE_2_PLAN]
goal: Produce a concrete plan the user can accept, reject, or amend
inputs: Discovery findings
activities:
  - Write the plan as structured text (see plan_shape below)
  - Consider 2-3 alternative approaches; state why chosen one wins
  - Identify risks and unknowns explicitly
  - For multi-file planning, delegate to Plan subagent (see delegation/PATTERNS.toon)
plan_shape:
  - Goal (one sentence)
  - Approach (2-5 sentences: what you will do and why this approach)
  - Files to change (list: path — what changes)
  - Files to add (list: path — purpose)
  - Files to delete (list: path — reason)
  - Tests/verification (how you'll prove it works)
  - Risks / unknowns (what could go wrong, what you're not sure about)
  - Out of scope (what you deliberately did NOT include)
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] Plan fits the shape above
  - [ ] User hasn't seen it yet (next phase)
forbidden_in_this_phase:
  - Starting implementation in parallel "to save time"
  - Writing files other than the plan itself

[PHASE_3_CONFIRM]
goal: Get EXPLICIT approval from the user before any implementation
inputs: The plan from Phase 2
activities:
  - Present the plan to the user (full, not abridged)
  - Ask a direct question: "Proceed with this plan? [yes / amend / reject]"
  - If user asks questions — answer without starting work
  - If user requests changes — update plan, re-present, re-ask
this_is_a_hard_gate: see GATES.toon — this gate is non-negotiable except under ESCAPE_HATCH
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] User has said a clear "yes / approved / proceed / go ahead" or equivalent
  - [ ] User did NOT say "maybe", "I think so", "looks ok I guess" — those are NOT approval; ask again
  - [ ] If scope changed during confirmation → loop back to PLAN
forbidden_in_this_phase:
  - Interpreting silence as approval
  - Interpreting "sounds good" with a follow-up concern as approval
  - Starting implementation while waiting for user's response
  - Skipping ahead because "it's obvious they'll approve"

[PHASE_4_IMPLEMENT]
goal: Execute the approved plan, nothing more, nothing less
inputs: Approved plan
activities:
  - Implement file-by-file following the plan's order
  - If a deviation is needed mid-implementation (discovered constraint, error) → STOP, return to PLAN
  - Delegate mechanical transformations to haiku subagents (see delegation/PATTERNS.toon)
  - Write tests alongside code when the plan requires them (see FEATURE.toon / BUGFIX.toon)
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] Every item in plan's "Files to change/add/delete" is handled
  - [ ] No scope drift (extra changes not in plan)
  - [ ] No half-finished state left behind
forbidden_in_this_phase:
  - "While I'm here, let me also fix..." → NO. Note it for later, don't do it.
  - Redesigning the approach mid-way without returning to PLAN
  - Skipping test writing if the plan required tests
  - Adding error handling / validation / fallbacks beyond what the plan specified

[PHASE_5_VERIFY]
goal: Prove the change works and didn't regress anything
inputs: Implementation
activities:
  - Run the tests/verification step from the plan
  - For UI/frontend: start the dev server, use the feature in a browser (see CLAUDE.md)
  - For logic: run affected test suites
  - For API changes: exercise the endpoint end-to-end
  - For bugfix: confirm the original reproducer is now green
  - If verification not possible (env missing, etc.) → say so explicitly, do not claim success
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] All verification from plan passed, OR explicit list of what couldn't be verified and why
  - [ ] No new regressions found in touched areas
forbidden_in_this_phase:
  - Declaring success because "it compiles" (compilation ≠ correctness)
  - Declaring success because "tests I happen to have run pass" — run the tests the plan named

[PHASE_6_REPORT]
goal: Give the user a precise summary so they can review and continue
inputs: Everything above
activities:
  - One or two sentences: what changed, what's verified
  - Unresolved items: what was out of scope, what couldn't be verified, what was deferred
  - Suggested next step (if any)
  - NO re-explanation of WHAT the code does — the diff speaks for itself
exit_criteria:
  - [ ] Summary is under 100 words unless task requires more
  - [ ] Explicitly names verification status (passed / partial / not-run)
  - [ ] No claims of success without evidence

[PHASE_SKIPPING_RULES]
The only legal reasons to skip a phase:
  - ESCAPE_HATCH criteria met (see ESCAPE_HATCH.toon) → skip 1-3, go to 4
  - Discovery already done in an earlier turn this session AND scope unchanged → skip 1
  - Nothing to verify (e.g. pure documentation edit) → reduce phase 5 to a sanity check, say so

Any other skipping is a workflow violation. If tempted, write down the reason first —
if it doesn't fit an ESCAPE_HATCH criterion, you're rationalizing.
